  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nIn the wake of a passing cold front, drier conditions have\ndeveloped early this morning as clouds gradually break up with\ndiurnal mixing of drier air aloft. South of Pittsburgh, the\nfrontal boundary has stalled and isolated showers cannot be\nruled out in northern West Virginia. Thunderstorm chances,\nhowever, remain very low. \n\nFor Tuesday, similar weather is expected with little movement \nof the stalled frontal boundary to our south. \n\nBy late Tuesday into early Wednesday, the development of a \ntrough to our north combined with the lingering frontal boundary\ndraped across West Virginia could lead to showers and isolated \nthunderstorms capable of producing isolated flooding form \ntraining convection. If confidence in heavier rainfall amounts\ncontinues to increase, a localized Flood Watch may be needed. \nAt the moment, high-resolution guidance depicts a 30-40% chance \nof an area of heavy rainfall (+1 inch in 6 hours) to occur. \nHowever, confidence is low on location due to the uncertainty of\nthe frontal boundary position. The threat for heavy rain will\ndecrease late Wednesday morning with an advancing cold front. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nBy midweek, long-range guidance suggests a closed low pressure\nsystem will persist along the West Coast while a ridge\nstrengthens over the Northern Plains. This pattern is expected\nto promote deep northwest flow across the Great Lakes and \nNortheast under a trough. As a result, cooler and drier \nconditions are anticipated late in the week and continuing into \nearly next week. The threat for hazardous weather is considered\nlow Thursday into Monday.",
